Date: Fri, 19 Nov 93 4:41:58 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Getting paid for Net surfing

R. David Murray:
> The other absolutely necessary things would be reputation data bases and
> an arbitration mechanism.  The structure of both of these are non-trivial
> (but definitely solvable) problems....

> So, anyone interested in discussing how to implement this?  

Sure, a bunch of us have been interested in this stuff for quite a
while.  We already have HEx and AMIX.  The best place to start might be
to figure out why these two projects haven't taken off, as the same 
fate likely awaits the next such project that does not learn the 
lessons from these.

The main criticisms of HEx have been (a) lack of market volume
(it's only available to Extropian list subscribers, only a small fraction
of whom are interested enough to participate), lack of anything 
"real" underlying reputation ratings, (c) lack of security, auditing,
etc. (which can perhaps be forgiven a startup operation).

> it ought to be possible to do it with very little capital outlay, 
> as a cooperative Net project.  

Many such projects are possible.  Some of the most successful direct
software projects (eg PGP and emacs) are based on idealistic, inspirational 
motivations of talented programmers with free time, rather than on a 
desire to make money.  The main reason for this is that you don't make 
much money by writing software and letting folks copy it; you can make 
money by hanging revenue generating operations off existing software 
(packaging, consulting, bundling, burning into ROMs, copyright and 
patent enforcement, on-line services, etc.) The vast majority of programmers 
make our living off such bundled or in-house applications, not by 
writing software for direct standalone sale.  Other pure-information 
forms, like music, suffer from a similar fate.  Most music is written 
and performed for mostly idealistic reasons, and the vast majority of 
money made off music is when the pure-info music is bundled with 
something else -- ad jingles, live performance (the music world's analog 
of online services like Dialog), and copyright enforcement via ASCAP.

> The best thing is, this would be the perfect test bed for positive
> reputations and competition in reputation/arbitration services. 

This is what HEx is supposed to be.  (Of course, there's no such
thing as a perfect test bed).  So we've already started testing
reputation services (and arbitration services with Harry's judgements);
the question now is how can we improve upon these?  

I agree that decentralization and starting small is a great way
to go.  Our little toys, the list  PPL and HEx, have contributed 
more to our understanding of these issues than, for example, AMIX, despite
the latter burning up far more time and money.  (Well, far more money
anyway :-)  Much like the comparison between SSC and a wider
diversity of physics experiments on a more reasonable scale.

One general observation: there are many very talented
people thinking abstractly about "how the net should look", what
software tools "should be used", etc., and relatively few people 
figuring out how we can solve practical business problems and meet 
large common demands via the net.  It's the people who
figure out how to meet practical demand via online net services
that will rake in the big $$$, not the people with the idealistic
visions.  (The latter might end up writing great, world-changing
software like PGP, but they won't make any $$$ off it!)

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

