From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: PHYSICS: Theory and experiment

Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu> writes:

>   Recently, I read about an interesting experiment:
>by cooling very rare gas (xenon, as I remember) to extremely low
>temperatures (about a millionth of a degree K), its atoms may be
>brought into state where they considerably overlap (the uncertainty
>principle states that for low [fluctuations] of energy - true for cold
>gas - we have fuzzy positions).  Multiple overlapping bosons create
>a new state of matter which seems to have no precedent in the history
>of physical Universe.
>  The original version of the equipment cost a few hundred dollars;
>now they are improving it to lower the temperature for the levels
>necessary for the predicted new state. Seem pretty close.
>  (Does anybody know any recent details on it?)
> 
>  Unlike SSC, this kind of research brings no satisfaction to macho/
>national feelings (our collider is bigger than yours! <E' dropped
>for quoting humans>), or digging contracts...
> 
>   I would expect that we have more things left in low-energy
>and low-$ physics; solid-state physics, including 'warm' superconductors,
>may bring us lots of interesting - and useful - results in the near
>future.

Indeed, it may be that SSC was never necessary in the first place.

New Scientist (18-Nov-1993) is a special issue focussing on plasma 
physics.  One of the articles covers some new multi-terawatt desktop
lasers (T3 lasers) that achieve higher energy density than enormous
Shiva-type lasers but fit on an optical bench. (They work by some kind
of phase-stretching mechanism; the laser pulse is stretched,
amplified, then compressed massively in the time domain so that it
comes out in something like 10e-14 seconds.) They're speculating about
using beat-wave pulses to accelerate electrons through a plasma cloud
to energies on the order of 100 GeV in a distance of 10 centimetres ... 

... instead of the 27Km track diameter of the LEP at CERN.

Indeed, if this actually can be made to work, it will bring particle
physics research back into the financial grasp of small university
departments, never mind the vast bureaucratic organizations like CERN
that have had a virtual monopoly on it since the 60's. I'm not sure
what the long term implications are, but it would be a great irony if
within months of the SSC being cancelled, similar accelerator energies
were being achieved by bench-top devices in universities.

Go check it out. Maybe some real physicists out there can tell the
rest of us if it's as good as it sounds.


-- Charlie
