It is a matter that calls for further research that on the conlang mailing
list or perhaps some other forum, perhaps Jacques Guy or some other person
posting from an account which perhaps resides at some point in the land
mass conventionally designated in English or perhaps some other language as
"Australia", that a language or other form of communication or verbal
behaviour conventionally designated as "Inoffensish" may already have been
invented or perhaps discovered or created, which might or might not bear
some resemblance or other connection of similarity or dissimilarity in
certain respects to C-Prime, by perhaps being intended to cause as little
offence as possible.

I'd say more, but the excessive length would itself offend, and thus break
the central principle of Inoffensish.  To really write in Inoffensish, one
must omit content both to keep the length down and to further reduce the
chances of offending anyone.  The ideal text in Inoffensish is a short
paragraph that says nothing at all.

As with E-Prime, writing in Inoffensish is a useful exercise, though by
contrasting means: exaggerating certain faults of writing, and hence of
thought, the better to notice them when writing or reading normally.

