Date: Fri, 19 Nov 1993 07:15:28 -0600 (CST)
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Uploads

I haven't seen much on Uploading here recently,
so here are a few random thoughts:

I wonder if the brain might be a classic case
of a complex system that is sensitive to
initial conditions and small changes in component
behavior.  On one hand, it makes some sense to
say (as Edelman essentially seems to) that the
important functions of the brain act on the
statistical aggregates of many neurons.  Making
a slightly imperfect copy of the original when
uploading, or not *perfectly* simulating the
physics of neurons (even if that is possible)
would then probably not make any difference at
all in behavior.  However, if (as with other
complex interacting systems) the resulting
behavior may in fact diverge quite quickly and
maybe even noticeably from what the original
would have done.

I know, I know -- big deal.  We change all the
time.  But now consider that as good Extropians
we believe that we are in competition for resources
and power with our peers.  It seems to me (all
points debatable, that's the point of posting!) that:

  * identity is linked to the fidelity of the
    neural simulation.  That is, the more inaccurate
    the neuron model, the more the "identity" of
    the upload will be the same as the identity
    of the original.  Note that Mark Miller's
    credit theory of identity denies this.  A
    wildly inaccurate upload, one sophisticated
    enough to do nothing but entertain a single
    thought "crediting" the original, would be
    at least as identity-preserving as a more
    "normal" upload that entertained some doubt.
    This is why I don't like the credit theory.

  * a functional brain simulation could be
    achieved with significant simplification of
    the neural model.  By "functional" I mean
    that the upload would work, but might behave
    very differently from the original.  Even
    more significant simplification could be
    derived from the approach that Moravec uses
    in Mind Children, which requires clever
    recoding of all brain functions to achieve
    the processing requirements he uses in his
    timetable estimates.

